# DURABLE REPRO — B4: `for x in &v: x = ...` over a SCALAR-element collection
# silently loses the write.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): prints
#     1 / 2 / 3
# The loop body runs, the assignment is accepted, and nothing reaches the
# collection. `gg check` is clean — silent-wrong-output.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): 11 / 12 / 13. `for x in &v` binds each element as a
# mutable borrow, which is the whole point of the sigil (D31 full-strict: `&`
# means the write goes through). The resource-element twins of this shape
# already write through — `cow_for_amp_resource_elem_writethrough.gg` — so this
# is the by-value half of the same rule, matching the by-value `&`-arg class in
# `sound_amp_byvalue_place_writethrough.gg`.
#
# ORACLE: out of ggdef's phase-0 subset ("`for &`/`for !` iteration is
# Increment B2"), so this row is pinned against the RULE, not against a third
# implementation. Noted here rather than left implicit — an out-of-subset shape
# owes an explicit note (Core #9).
#
# The written value depends on the element read (`x + 10`), so a const-fold
# cannot elide it, and the three distinct results prove every iteration landed
# rather than one write being smeared.
void main():
    Vector[int] v = [1, 2, 3]
    for x in &v:
        x = x + 10
    print(v[0])
    print(v[1])
    print(v[2])
